      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.stselectronicrecyclinginc.com/bakersfield-ca#faqpage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What standards support your data handling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS programs can be structured around NIST 800-88, HIPAA, ISO 27001, and DoD 5220.22-M reference requirements. Project documentation is matched to equipment, client policy, and the information handled by each Bakersfield organization. These standards help define sanitization methods, access controls, documentation practices, and review procedures for retired technology."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What areas do you serve?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS serves Bakersfield, Kern County, and surrounding Central Valley communities through scheduled commercial pickup. Service planning can accommodate offices, farms, warehouses, schools, healthcare campuses, and public-sector sites. Organizations searching for electronics recycling near me in Bakersfield can request pickup planning for Oildale, Rosedale, Shafter, and other Kern County service areas."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does pickup take?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pickup timing depends on equipment volume, site access, transportation needs, and scheduling availability. Many commercial projects in Bakersfield can be arranged within the same week after the scope and collection details are confirmed. STS reviews the equipment list, quantity, loading details, and distance before confirming the service plan."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens to equipment after pickup?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Equipment is received, recorded, sorted, and directed toward reuse, recovery, or destruction based on its condition and data requirements. Clients receive documentation that supports internal asset and environmental reporting. Bakersfield organizations can request asset inventories, certificates of destruction, weight reports, and downstream summaries according to the selected service."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is pickup free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pickup may be available at no charge for qualifying volumes and project conditions in Bakersfield and Kern County. STS reviews the equipment list, quantity, loading details, distance, and scheduling requirements before confirming the service plan."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you provide audit documentation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Available records may include pickup documentation, asset inventories, certificates of destruction, weight reports, and downstream summaries. The final documentation package depends on the selected service and project scope. These records help Bakersfield businesses, institutions, and agencies retain evidence for internal review and audits."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does the electronics recycling process work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The process begins with project review, including equipment types, estimated volume, site access, timing, and documentation needs. Pickup records and asset details establish custody from collection through downstream processing. Equipment is then received, recorded, sorted, and directed toward reuse, recovery, or destruction, with project documentation provided according to the selected service."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How is information protected during electronics recycling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Retired computers, servers, phones, and storage devices may retain business, patient, student, or operational information. STS coordinates documented erasure and physical destruction options based on media type, project requirements, and client policy. For healthcare projects, HIPAA and 45 CFR §164.312 provide reference points for safeguarding electronic protected health information."
          }
        }
      ]
